<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The texture graph's evaluator: a plan of compute kernels over a pool of intermediate
            images, dispatched on a graphics device and read back as pixels. No graph, no document,
            no panel — the artefact both front ends compile to, and the thing that runs it.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>editor;texture-graph;material-authoring;compute;raven;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>

        <!--
          ⚠ **A workaround for a name-resolution collision, and it should go the moment two words
          change in `TexturePlanEvaluator.cs`.** That file writes `Core.Imaging.Bitmap` and
          `Core.Imaging.PngCodec` — relative names, resolved by walking out from
          `namespace Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph` until something called `Core` turns up. Until the
          `Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph` reference below, the first hit was `Vixen.Core`; that reference
          brings `Vixen.Editor.Core` in transitively, `Vixen.Editor` is a *nearer* enclosing namespace
          than `Vixen`, and the same two lines then name a namespace with no `Imaging` in it. Nothing
          in either file changed and the build stopped.

          ⚠ **A `global using Core = Vixen.Core;` does not fix it** — tried, and it does not: a
          compilation-unit alias is consulted only after the enclosing-namespace walk, so
          `Vixen.Editor.Core` still wins. Spelling `Vixen.Core.Imaging.Bitmap` in that one file is the
          real fix, and this slice does not own it — [#717](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/717).

          What this property buys beyond dodging that: the compile-time reference set becomes exactly
          what is named below, which is why `Vixen.Core` and `Vixen.Core.Mathematics` had to be added
          — they used to arrive through `Vixen.Graphics`. It does *not* keep the interface framework
          out of the output directory, which the note on the node-graph reference is about.
        -->
        <DisableTransitiveProjectReferences>true</DisableTransitiveProjectReferences>

        <!--
          CA1051 (no visible instance fields) is the one rule a node library cannot keep, for the
          reason `Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph` states: a node's ports *are* its fields, which is what
          lets the generator read the declaration once. The nodes here are internal, so the rule does
          not fire today; the line is here so that widening one for a panel is not also a build
          failure nobody predicted.
        -->
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1051</NoWarn>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          ⚠ The kernels are embedded rather than compiled to a committed `.spv` beside the source,
          and that is a deliberate departure from `Editor/Vixen.Editor.Host/Shaders`. A storage
          image's texel format is part of its *type* in both SPIR-V and GLSL, and Raven's
          `[Permutation]` values are bool, int and uint — so one kernel that writes `rgba8` in one
          plan and `rgba16f` in the next cannot be one module. The variants are produced from this
          text at load, which means there is no committed binary anybody can leave stale, and
          `CheckShaders`' inventory asks for an entry only where one exists.
        -->
        <EmbeddedResource Include="Shaders\*.rvn" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          A device to allocate images on and dispatch over, the effect model that says what a
          compiled variant binds, and the in-process Raven compiler the editor already resolves its
          own variants through — the same three `Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph` takes, for the same
          reason: a device is not a UI.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Graphics\Vixen.Graphics.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Shaders\Vixen.Shaders.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Tools\Vixen.ShaderCompiler\Vixen.ShaderCompiler.csproj" />

        <!--
          ⚠ The Raven *front end*, on top of the effect compiler above, and the two answer different
          questions. `RavenEffectCompiler` compiles a whole shader to bytecode and throws a string on
          failure; doc 48 § D6 needs Raven's own `Diagnostic` objects — with a `Location` a line
          number comes out of — so that a complaint about generated text names the node whose
          expression is on that line, and it needs `FieldSymbol.ConstantValue` so that a scalar
          parameter's expression is folded by the real compiler rather than by a second evaluator
          this repository would then own. `Vixen.Editor.AssetEditors` takes the same reference for the
          same first reason. It is already here at runtime, transitively through the effect compiler;
          this only makes it visible to the compilation.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Raven\Vixen.Raven\Vixen.Raven.csproj" />

        <!-- And the syntax layer a `Diagnostic` and its `Location` are declared in, which
             `DisableTransitiveProjectReferences` above stops arriving through Raven itself. -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Syntax\Vixen.Core.Syntax.csproj" />

        <!--
          And the image containers, because a bake's last step is a file. `PngCodec` sits under
          everything that writes a picture in this repository precisely so that a new writer does
          not become a new encoder.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Imaging\Vixen.Core.Imaging.csproj" />

        <!--
          ⚠ Named rather than inherited, and that is what `DisableTransitiveProjectReferences` above
          costs: `Int2`, `Color4` and `CurveSample` used to arrive through `Vixen.Graphics`.
          `Vixen.Core` is where `Vixen.Core.Curves` lives, which is not obvious from the namespace.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Mathematics\Vixen.Core.Mathematics.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core\Vixen.Core.csproj" />

        <!--
          And the node-graph framework, which M1 deliberately left out and M4 cannot do without: a
          `TextureGraphCompiler` is a `NodeGraphCompiler<TexturePlan>`, and there is no way to derive
          from a type without referencing the assembly that declares it.

          ⚠ **What the omission bought is gone, and it was worth something.** This assembly's README
          says "does not reference `Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph` at all, which is what makes every test
          here a test of the evaluator" — after this line no test proves that by construction, and a
          plan-shaped assertion that quietly went through a node would look identical. What replaces
          it is a convention rather than a wall: the compiler suites build their own graphs and the
          evaluator suites still hand-build their plans, and the one differential test that crosses
          the line compares the two.

          ⚠ **And it makes this a UI assembly at runtime.** `Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph` references
          `Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced`, `Vixen.Editor.Core` and `Vixen.Editor.Inspector` — its own
          csproj says so at length — so `Vixen.Ui.dll` is now in this assembly's output directory and
          anything that loads the evaluator loads the interface framework, including the content build
          that M5 will run headless. `DisableTransitiveProjectReferences` narrows what this project
          *compiles* against and does nothing about that. Splitting the compiler into an assembly of
          its own is the fix — [#720](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/720).
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.csproj" />

        <!--
          ⚠ **Doc 48 § 4.6's `Normal → Height` is a grid Poisson, and doc 42 § B1's solver is the
          solver.** `Vixen.Geometry.Uv/Solving` is a general sparse `SparseMatrix` /
          `ConjugateGradient` pair that happens to live behind an unwrapper's front door — warm
          started, with a fixed iteration budget rather than a residual test, which is exactly the
          determinism a bake wants. Its types are `internal`, so that assembly names this one in an
          `InternalsVisibleTo`; the wart and its fix are recorded there and in
          [#755](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/755).

          ⚠ **`Vixen.Core.Threading` is not optional even though nothing here starts a thread.**
          `ConjugateGradient.Solve` takes a `JobScheduler?` with a default of null, and C# cannot
          bind a call to a method whose parameter type the compilation cannot see — so leaving it out
          is a build error at the call site rather than a reference nobody uses. It costs two
          assemblies in the output directory, measured: `Vixen.Geometry` and `Vixen.Core.Threading`
          were the only names the closure did not already hold.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Geometry.Uv\Vixen.Geometry.Uv.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Threading\Vixen.Core.Threading.csproj" />

        <!--
          ⚠ **The text stack, for doc 48 § 4.1's `Text`, and it costs nothing in the output
          directory.** Batch 5 refused `Core/Vixen.Ui` on a closure measurement — 20 projects against
          this assembly's 17 — and that measurement is now stale in both columns. Re-derived over
          every `ProjectReference` in the tree on 2026-09-05: this assembly's closure is **29**
          projects and **`Vixen.Ui` and `Vixen.Ui.Text` are already two of them**, arriving through
          the `Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph` reference above (→ `Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced` → `Vixen.Ui`
          → `Vixen.Ui.Text`), whose own comment says as much. `Vixen.Ui`'s closure is 14 and a strict
          subset of those 29. So naming either adds **zero** files to `bin/`; what it changes is only
          what this compilation may spell, which `DisableTransitiveProjectReferences` above otherwise
          hides.

          Only the text half is named, because only the text half is used. Glyph outlines and a
          scanline fill are `Vixen.Ui.Text`; `SvgPath` and `PathFlattener` are `Vixen.Ui` proper, and
          § 4.1's `Svg Path` stays unwritten for a reason that is about a rasteriser rather than
          about a reference — [#756](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/756).
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ui.Text\Vixen.Ui.Text.csproj" />

        <!--
          The node generator, over this assembly's own [Node] classes. Analyzers do not flow through
          a ProjectReference, so every assembly declaring nodes references it directly — which is
          also what a third-party plugin does.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.Generator\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
